WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:08.567
In Lecture Six, we computed a power in two ways, and saw on the call stack that one of them is much faster.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.580
Now we can say exactly how much faster.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:20.290
We'll count recursive functions with one simple idea: the work per call, times the number of calls.

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:26.427
This is Lecture Nine, part three: analysing recursion, and binary search.

00:00:26.777 --> 00:00:33.737
We'll count the calls of power, fast power and fib, and then prove that binary search takes logarithmic time.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.285
Power goes from n to n minus one.

00:00:37.285 --> 00:00:39.702
If n is zero, it returns one.

00:00:39.702 --> 00:00:44.454
Otherwise, it returns x times power of x and n minus one.

00:00:44.804 --> 00:00:54.084
Power of x and five calls power with four, three, two, one, and zero: n plus one calls in all.

00:00:54.434 --> 00:01:01.723
Each call does a constant amount of work: a comparison, a multiplication, a call and a return.

00:01:01.723 --> 00:01:07.074
So the total is a constant times n plus one, and power is big-O of n.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.842
Fast power goes from n to n halved.

00:01:10.842 --> 00:01:19.427
It computes partial, the power for half of n, squares it, and if n is odd, multiplies by x once more.

00:01:19.777 --> 00:01:30.210
The slide counts one call, line by line: one for the test, one for the return, two each for the three lines with arithmetic, and one for the final return.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:36.577
At most eight operations, plus the recursive call, which the slide marks with a question mark.

00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:40.521
The question mark is the same question on a smaller input.

00:01:40.521 --> 00:01:47.647
Every call does at most a constant amount of work itself, so the total is that constant, times the number of calls.

00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:50.882
And each call cuts n in half.

00:01:50.882 --> 00:01:57.847
A call with n equal to 13 calls itself with 6, then 3, then 1, then 0.

00:01:57.847 --> 00:01:59.637
Five calls in all.

00:01:59.987 --> 00:02:03.991
This function counts the calls for any n.

00:02:03.991 --> 00:02:05.944
For 1,000, 11 calls.

00:02:05.944 --> 00:02:10.436
For 1,000,000, 21 calls, instead of 1,000,000.

00:02:10.436 --> 00:02:14.635
That's the floor of log two of n, plus two.

00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:17.467
Fast power is big-O of log n.

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:26.630
Now fib, written straight from the definition: fib of n is fib of n minus one, plus fib of n minus two.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.147
And fib calls counts its calls.

00:02:29.497 --> 00:02:32.766
Fib calls of 20 is 21,891.

00:02:32.766 --> 00:02:40.937
Pause the video and predict: what are fib calls of 21, and of 22?

00:02:41.487 --> 00:02:45.005
35,421, and 57,313.

00:02:45.005 --> 00:02:54.447
Each step of n multiplies the calls by about 1.618.

00:02:54.797 --> 00:02:56.037
We can bound it.

00:02:56.037 --> 00:03:04.561
Fib of n calls both fib of n minus one and fib of n minus two, so the count more than doubles every two steps.

00:03:04.561 --> 00:03:11.303
And each call makes at most two calls, so the count is less than two to the n plus one.

00:03:11.303 --> 00:03:14.557
Fib is exponential: big-O of two to the n.

00:03:14.907 --> 00:03:18.322
Fib of 50 would take about 40 billion calls.

00:03:18.322 --> 00:03:23.056
The fix is to stop computing the same values again and again.

00:03:23.056 --> 00:03:27.479
This loop keeps the last two numbers, and makes one pass.

00:03:27.479 --> 00:03:31.747
It's big-O of n, and fib loop of 50 comes back at once.

00:03:32.097 --> 00:03:34.729
So recursion itself isn't slow.

00:03:34.729 --> 00:03:38.039
Fast power is recursive, and very fast.

00:03:38.039 --> 00:03:43.217
Fib is slow because its two calls solve overlapping problems.

00:03:44.001 --> 00:03:47.874
Binary search, from Lecture Six, searches a sorted list.

00:03:47.874 --> 00:03:52.093
The candidates are the elements from index low to index high.

00:03:52.093 --> 00:03:53.961
It looks at the middle one.

00:03:54.311 --> 00:03:57.665
If the target equals the middle element, it's found.

00:03:57.665 --> 00:04:00.696
If the target is smaller, search the left half.

00:04:00.696 --> 00:04:03.147
If it's larger, search the right half.

00:04:03.147 --> 00:04:06.631
And if no candidates are left, the target isn't there.

00:04:06.981 --> 00:04:10.697
Let's search this list of 9 numbers for 7.

00:04:10.697 --> 00:04:12.201
The middle is 16.

00:04:12.201 --> 00:04:15.741
Seven is smaller, so keep the left half.

00:04:16.091 --> 00:04:17.752
The middle is now 3.

00:04:17.752 --> 00:04:20.826
Seven is larger: keep the right part.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.571
Then 5: larger again.

00:04:22.571 --> 00:04:24.315
Then 6: larger again.

00:04:24.315 --> 00:04:27.971
No candidates are left, so it returns False.

00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:31.211
It looked at four elements out of nine.

00:04:32.001 --> 00:04:34.881
Why is binary search big-O of log n?

00:04:34.881 --> 00:04:42.641
Each call does a constant amount of work besides its one recursive call, so again we count calls.

00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:46.967
The number of candidates is high minus low plus one.

00:04:46.967 --> 00:04:53.544
The next call keeps only the candidates left of the middle, or only those right of it.

00:04:53.544 --> 00:04:55.991
Either way, that's at most half.

00:04:56.341 --> 00:05:03.744
So there are n candidates in the first call, at most half of n in the second, at most a quarter in the third.

00:05:03.744 --> 00:05:07.141
Every call halves them again, until none are left.

00:05:07.491 --> 00:05:18.817
In the worst case the target isn't there, and the number of calls that look at an element is the floor of log two of n, plus one.

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:21.891
So binary search is big-O of log n.

00:05:22.668 --> 00:05:25.015
Let's check the proof by counting.

00:05:25.015 --> 00:05:29.572
Linear search counts how many elements sequential search looks at.

00:05:29.572 --> 00:05:32.748
Binary search does the same for binary search.

00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:39.266
On the numbers 0 to 999, searching for 1,000, which isn't there,

00:05:39.266 --> 00:05:42.832
sequential search looks at all 1,000.

00:05:42.832 --> 00:05:45.338
Binary search looks at 10.

00:05:45.688 --> 00:05:50.258
Searching for 0, sequential search is lucky: one look.

00:05:50.258 --> 00:05:55.928
Binary search needs 9, because 0 is at the far end of its halvings.

00:05:56.278 --> 00:05:59.222
In the worst case, the target is missing.

00:05:59.222 --> 00:06:01.519
This table goes up to 1,000,000.

00:06:01.519 --> 00:06:04.392
Sequential search makes 1,000,000 looks.

00:06:04.392 --> 00:06:10.998
Binary search makes 20, and the last column, the formula from the proof, matches it exactly.

00:06:11.348 --> 00:06:14.839
One catch: binary search needs sorted data.

00:06:14.839 --> 00:06:18.899
On unsorted data, it silently gives wrong answers.

00:06:18.899 --> 00:06:22.148
So how do we sort? That's the next part.

00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:24.135
Let's sum up.

00:06:24.485 --> 00:06:30.205
To analyse a recursive function, multiply the work per call by the number of calls.

00:06:30.555 --> 00:06:35.962
Cutting the problem in half at each call gives about log n calls:

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:39.955
fast power and binary search are big-O of log n.

00:06:40.305 --> 00:06:44.403
Fib's two calls overlap, so its calls grow exponentially.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:46.345
A loop makes it big-O of n.

00:06:46.695 --> 00:06:48.144
Here's one to try.

00:06:48.144 --> 00:06:54.344
Write fast power mults, which counts the multiplications fast power performs.

00:06:54.344 --> 00:06:56.357
For 13, you should get 7.

00:06:56.357 --> 00:06:59.015
How does that compare with power?

00:06:59.565 --> 00:07:06.405
In part four, we'll sort, with bubble sort and quick sort, and count how much work each one does.

